============================================================
CONTINUITY FLAGS (manuscript vs. files)
============================================================
- Travel time: Maedelyn says "four days", Valia says "four days" at the Duke's town, but Aethres opens with "Five days on the road." Possibly the extra day came from Rinnie's early halts, but it isn't stated.
- The Rinnie sheet says the parents "died to disease" and she was raised in "the Stumps." The manuscript says the guards burned the parents' street during a coughing-sickness outbreak, and the slum is "the Greys." The Dusty sheet matches the manuscript (guards, Greys).
- Valia's sheet has skirts, bare legs, mismatched boots, and her father's robe "always" worn. The manuscript has a green cloak, a high-neck blouse, baggy pants, and heeled boots, with the robe kept folded. The sheet palette is blue/black/brown, but the cloak is green.
- Student count: the manuscript (4-5k) matches the Lore Bible (~5,000). The Antonaez sheet's sample line says "two thousand students."
- In the Aethres street scene, the dialogue tag is doubled ("Valia nudged her horse close to his... Valia called over the din, nudging her horse closer to his"). Left unedited.
- Pity Lottery: the Lore Bible has ~10 impoverished children with limited abilities per year, tuition fully paid (~50 across the five-year program). The manuscript has "a few common-born students every year" and 300+ non-nobles overall via talent, noble sponsorship, or random clergy picks. These are compatible if the lottery is the clergy-pick route and makes up a small slice of the 300, but the text doesn't pin down which route is "the lottery." The Lore Bible also says that Dusty is one of the rare few who rise.
- Lore Bible: the Solarium (infirmary wing, open air, greenery, Maid-enforced neutrality) is "the one place his status does not matter." It isn't introduced yet.
- Dusty's sheet says Valia spent "her one institutional favor"; in the text she believes Antonaez always says yes (consistent with her obliviousness).
